  <section id="what" class="container-tight pb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink-900">1. What a heat pump actually is</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-ink-700">
      A heat pump is a single piece of equipment that does the work of a furnace <em>and</em> an air conditioner. The breakthrough is that it doesn't <strong>create</strong> heat by burning a fuel — it <strong>moves</strong> heat from one place to another using a refrigerant cycle, the same physics that runs your refrigerator. In winter the system pulls heat out of the outdoor air (yes, even at sub-freezing temperatures — there's still latent heat in cold air down to roughly &minus;15&deg;F for cold-climate units) and releases it indoors. In summer the cycle runs in reverse and the indoor heat gets dumped outside, which is just air conditioning. One machine, two seasons, no combustion.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-ink-700">
      Because moving heat takes far less energy than making it, a modern heat pump delivers 2.5&ndash;4&times; the heating output of the electricity it consumes. That ratio is the <strong>Coefficient of Performance (COP)</strong>. A 95% AFUE gas furnace tops out at COP &asymp; 0.95. A baseboard electric heater is COP = 1.0. A reasonable air-source heat pump in a moderate climate averages COP &asymp; 3.0 across a heating season. That's why heat pumps cut bills against oil, propane, and electric-resistance heat in every state, and against natural gas in roughly two-thirds of states once you account for current retail prices.
    </p>

